🧰 Core Java Command-Line Tools

These tools — like javac, javap, jar, javadoc, jdb, etc. — are part of the:

✅ JDK (Java Development Kit)



1. 🛠️ javac — Java Compiler
Usage: Compiles .java files to .class bytecode.

Example:


javac HelloWorld.java
Output: HelloWorld.class

2. 🔎 javap — Java Class File Disassembler
Usage: Displays bytecode-level method signatures and other class details.

Example:


javap HelloWorld
or to see method internals:


javap -c HelloWorld
Use case: Debugging compiled code, checking bytecode, reverse-engineering classes

3. 🧪 java — Java Runtime Launcher
Usage: Runs a compiled .class file or JAR.

Example:


java HelloWorld
4. 📦 jar — Java Archive Tool
Usage: Creates and manages .jar files (Java archives).

Example:


jar cf app.jar *.class
cf = create, file name

5. 🧬 javadoc — Documentation Generator
Usage: Generates HTML documentation from Javadoc comments.

Example:


javadoc MyClass.java
6. 🕵️‍♂️ jdb — Java Debugger
Usage: Command-line debugger for Java programs.

Example:


jdb HelloWorld
7. 📈 jconsole, jvisualvm, jstat, jmap, jstack
These tools are for monitoring, profiling, and debugging running Java applications:

| Tool        | Purpose                              |
| ----------- | ------------------------------------ |
| `jconsole`  | GUI monitoring (heap, threads, etc.) |
| `jvisualvm` | Advanced profiling & monitoring      |
| `jstack`    | Prints thread dump                   |
| `jmap`      | Prints memory map (heap info)        |
| `jstat`     | JVM statistics (GC, class loading)   |
